@rem Exercises for lesson 04_classes (C++ track). Format: docs/CONTENT_FORMAT.md

@exercise cpp-class-theory-method
@type theory
@lesson 04_classes
@difficulty 1
@prompt
What is a function that belongs to a class called?
@answer
method | a method | member function | a member function
@explain
A method is called on an object (vec.print()) and works on that object's fields.

@exercise cpp-class-theory-default-access
@type theory
@lesson 04_classes
@difficulty 1
@prompt
What is the default access of the members of a class (not a struct)? Write public or private.
@answer
private
@explain
In a class the members are private by default; in a struct they are public by default.

@exercise cpp-class-choice-copy-ctor
@type choice
@lesson 04_classes
@difficulty 2
@prompt
Which is the correct declaration of the copy constructor of class Point?
@choices
- Point(Point o);
* Point(const Point& o);
- Point(const Point o);
- Point& Point(const Point& o);
@explain
The copy constructor takes a const reference to another object of the same class. Taking the parameter by value would need a copy to make the copy.

@exercise cpp-class-choice-static
@type choice
@lesson 04_classes
@difficulty 2
@prompt
What can a static method NOT use?
@choices
- Its parameters
- Other static members
* this and the non-static members
- Local variables
@explain
A static member belongs to the class itself, not to an object, so there is no this.

@exercise cpp-class-choice-shallow
@type choice
@lesson 04_classes
@difficulty 2
@prompt
A class has a char* name field and no copy constructor. What does  MITStudent b = a;  do to the pointer?
@choices
- It makes b.name point to a new copy of the text
* It copies the address, so a.name and b.name point to the same text
- It sets b.name to nullptr
- It does not compile
@explain
The default copy constructor copies every field. For a pointer field that copies the address (a shallow copy), so changing one object changes the other.

@exercise cpp-class-predict-ctors
@type predict
@lesson 04_classes
@difficulty 2
@prompt
What does this program print?
@code
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Point {
public:
    double x, y;
    Point() : x(0), y(0) { cout << "default" << endl; }
    Point(double nx, double ny) : x(nx), y(ny) { cout << "two" << endl; }
};

int main() {
    Point p;
    Point q(1, 2);
    cout << p.x << "," << q.y << endl;
    return 0;
}
@answer
default
two
0,2
@explain
Each constructor prints its message when the object is created; then p.x is 0 and q.y is 2.

@exercise cpp-class-predict-destructor
@type predict
@lesson 04_classes
@difficulty 2
@prompt
What does this program print?
@code
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class T {
public:
    T() { cout << "A "; }
    ~T() { cout << "B "; }
};

int main() {
    {
        T t;
        cout << "in ";
    }
    cout << "out" << endl;
    return 0;
}
@answer
A in B out
@explain
t is created (A), the block prints "in ", t goes out of scope at the closing brace and its destructor prints B, then "out".

@exercise cpp-class-predict-shallow
@type predict
@lesson 04_classes
@difficulty 3
@prompt
What does this program print?
@code
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class MITStudent {
public:
    int studentID;
    char* name;
    MITStudent() : studentID(0), name(nullptr) { }
};

int main() {
    MITStudent student1;
    student1.studentID = 98;
    char n[] = "foo";
    student1.name = n;

    MITStudent student2 = student1;
    student2.name[0] = 'b';
    cout << student1.name << endl;
    return 0;
}
@answer
boo
@explain
The default copy copies the pointer, not the text. student2.name and student1.name point to the same array, so student1 changes too.

@exercise cpp-class-predict-alias
@type predict
@lesson 04_classes
@difficulty 2
@prompt
What does this program print?
@code
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Point {
public:
    double x, y;
    Point() : x(0.0), y(0.0) { }
};

int main() {
    Point* q = new Point();
    Point* alias = q;
    alias->x = 10;
    q->y = 20;
    cout << q->x << " " << q->y << endl;
    delete q;
    return 0;
}
@answer
10 20
@explain
alias and q point to the same object, so a change through one is visible through the other.

@exercise cpp-class-fill-init-list
@type fill
@lesson 04_classes
@difficulty 1
@prompt
Complete the constructor with an initialization list.
@code
class Point {
public:
    double x, y;
    Point(double nx, double ny) : x(___), y(___) { }
};
@answer
nx
ny
@explain
The initialization list sets the fields before the constructor body runs.

@exercise cpp-class-fill-copy-ctor
@type fill
@lesson 04_classes
@difficulty 3
@prompt
Complete the copy constructor so that it makes a deep copy of the name.
@code
class MITStudent {
public:
    int studentID;
    char* name;

    MITStudent(int id, const char* n) : studentID(id) {
        name = new char[strlen(n) + 1];
        strcpy(name, n);
    }
    MITStudent(const MITStudent& o) : studentID(o.studentID) {
        name = new char[strlen(o.name) + 1];
        strcpy(name, ___);
    }
    ~MITStudent() { delete[] name; }
};
@answer
o.name
@explain
The copy constructor allocates its own array and copies the text from the other object's name.

@exercise cpp-class-fill-static
@type fill
@lesson 04_classes
@difficulty 2
@prompt
A static data member must be defined once outside the class. Fill in the class name.
@code
class Maths {
public:
    static float PI;
};

float ___::PI = 3.14f;
@answer
Maths
@explain
ClassName::member tells the compiler which class the definition belongs to.

@exercise cpp-class-bug-semicolon
@type bug
@lesson 04_classes
@difficulty 1
@prompt
This code does not compile. Click the buggy line, then type the corrected line.
@code
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Point {
public:
    double x, y;
}

int main() {
    Point p;
    p.x = 1.0;
    cout << p.x << endl;
    return 0;
}
@bugline 7
@answer
};
@explain
A class definition ends with a semicolon after the closing brace.

@exercise cpp-class-bug-private
@type bug
@lesson 04_classes
@difficulty 2
@prompt
This code does not compile. Click the line that causes the error.
@code
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Point {
private:
    double x;
public:
    Point(double nx) : x(nx) { }
};

int main() {
    Point p(2.0);
    cout << p.x << endl;
    return 0;
}
@bugline 13
@explain
x is private, so it cannot be read from outside the class. Add a public getter or make the field public.
